<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf of Izmir</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gulf of Smyrna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf of Izmir is an inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a> on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, reaching far inland, with the city of Izmir, ancient Smyrna, at its head. Its sheltered waters made Smyrna one of the chief ports of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> from antiquity to the Ottoman period. The Gediz, the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediz-river">Hermus</a>, enters the gulf on its northern shore.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-izmir.jpg" alt="Gulf of Izmir" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gulf_of_%C4%B0zmir.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
